1
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:09,369
Rym: It's Wednesday, August 14th, 2024.

2
00:00:09,369 --> 00:00:09,630
Rym: I'm Rym.

3
00:00:12,422 --> 00:00:12,838
Scott: I'm Scott.

4
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:14,246
Rym: And this is GeekNights.

5
00:00:14,306 --> 00:00:16,920
Rym: Tonight, we are reviewing Scavengers Reign.

6
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:18,639
Scott: Let's do this!

7
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:30,340
Rym: So, uh, way back, you may remember that Kickstarter, which we were big fans of, they made possibly the stupidest announcement that any company that we've ever liked has ever made.

8
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:40,380
Rym: They announced that they were embracing the blockchain, and then they announced a bunch of word salad because the blockchain's fake and a scam, about how they were going to use the fake scam blockchain to do nothing.

9
00:00:40,982 --> 00:00:43,800
Scott: They said the word "blockchain" and provided no specifics.

10
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:48,639
Scott: And then, they clearly have abandoned all those plans by now.

11
00:00:49,781 --> 00:00:53,479
Rym: Though they still have not, like, formally repudiated them.

12
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:54,323
Scott: Exactly.

13
00:00:54,343 --> 00:01:01,560
Scott: They have not, basically, have just remained silent on the matter since, and have just operated quietly as business as usual.

14
00:01:02,262 --> 00:01:07,020
Scott: It's clear they're not doing any of that, but they also haven't said that they're not doing any of that.

15
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:09,572
Scott: But they also haven't said anything about it since.

16
00:01:09,632 --> 00:01:11,180
Scott: They just have said nothing.

17
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:16,435
Rym: And that's part of why, like, at the time, I literally was like, "Well, I'm done.

18
00:01:16,475 --> 00:01:17,578
Rym: Boycott Kickstarter.

19
00:01:17,639 --> 00:01:17,819
Rym: Fuck

20
00:01:17,839 --> 00:01:18,060
Rym: them.".

21
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:19,004
Rym: Like, I walked away.

22
00:01:20,329 --> 00:01:22,800
Rym: And the fact that I actually never went back, partly because of what you said.

23
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:26,660
Rym: They've never addressed the fact that they fell for and promoted a scam.

24
00:01:27,001 --> 00:01:30,092
Scott: Like, if you've abandoned it, how hard is it?

25
00:01:30,132 --> 00:01:31,457
Scott: you could just put out one thing?

26
00:01:31,497 --> 00:01:32,360
Scott: Like, "Yeah, we're not doing

27
00:01:32,380 --> 00:01:32,460
Scott: that.".

28
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:38,140
Rym: And I suspect they're afraid to say that because then the Bitcoin people are going to go after them and they don't want to deal with it.

29
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:39,700
Rym: Because the Bitcoin people are all stupid.

30
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:40,987
Rym: I don't know.

31
00:01:41,231 --> 00:01:41,660
Scott: But anyway.

32
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:56,380
Rym: So anyway, at the time, while I was 100% done with Kickstarter and I literally never went back, I left my account in place because at the time I had two projects that were nearing completion that had not shipped to me yet.

33
00:01:56,663 --> 00:01:57,800
Rym: That I'd backed previously.

34
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:05,940
Rym: So all I did was basically change my name in Kickstarter to be a sort of protest thing.

35
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:08,479
Rym: And I changed my profile to be an anti-Bitcoin thing.

36
00:02:09,321 --> 00:02:11,772
Rym: And then I just logged out and never logged back in.

37
00:02:11,812 --> 00:02:13,420
Rym: And I didn't think about it again.

38
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:20,920
Rym: So today, out of nowhere, I got an email from Kickstarter because of a project I had backed a long time ago.

39
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:22,167
Rym: Because I'd forgotten about this.

40
00:02:22,207 --> 00:02:23,840
Rym: Like, I didn't even think about this account.

41
00:02:24,001 --> 00:02:25,159
Rym: I never planned to log into it again.

42
00:02:25,862 --> 00:02:34,519
Rym: And I forgot that I had changed my name, like my account profile name in Kickstarter, to be a little protest message on my way out the door.

43
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:39,802
Rym: So I got an email that literally opens, from Kickstarter officially, "Hi!

44
00:02:39,922 --> 00:02:41,209
Rym: Blockchain is a scam!

45
00:02:41,792 --> 00:02:42,054
Rym: Quit

46
00:02:42,154 --> 00:02:42,717
Rym: Kickstarter!".

47
00:02:44,603 --> 00:02:45,918
Rym: Because that's what my name was.

48
00:02:47,841 --> 00:02:54,200
Rym: And it's just kind of, it's sad that a company like that that was doing so well, like, it wasn't even that they fell for a scam.

49
00:02:54,340 --> 00:03:01,020
Rym: It seems like some executives in the company were scammers trying to make the blockchain scam a thing.

50
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:07,220
Scott: Nobody, it's such a weird, you know, it's like something that seemed like it was going in all the right directions.

51
00:03:07,341 --> 00:03:10,578
Scott: Got really weird and now has stayed mysterious.

52
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:12,082
Rym: Yup.

53
00:03:12,142 --> 00:03:19,980
Rym: And meanwhile, almost every creator I know who relied on Kickstarter that I cared about a lot, they all bailed on Kickstarter about that same time.

54
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:24,780
Rym: And now like all the indie RPGs have different channels and like I don't even need Kickstarter anymore.

55
00:03:24,981 --> 00:03:26,900
Rym: Everything I would ever want is on other platforms.

56
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:30,960
Rym: In fact, a lot of stuff I'm buying directly from BackerKit.

57
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:42,000
Scott: I mean, you know, it's just, I feel like, you know, that was already at that time, like, you know, when Kickstarter first started, there were so few projects.

58
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:47,518
Rym: Yeah, like I would go to Kickstarter every week or so and look like, "Ooh, what new wallets are people Kickstarting

59
00:03:47,558 --> 00:03:47,719
Rym: today?".

60
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:50,060
Scott: Well, because there would only be like five new things, right?

61
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:50,501
Scott: Yup.

62
00:03:50,542 --> 00:03:52,240
Rym: And it'd be like wallets and keychains mostly.

63
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:53,041
Scott: Right.

64
00:03:53,061 --> 00:04:00,520
Scott: And then there was a time period where, you know, there were more projects, but people were actually doing things where they wouldn't do them.

65
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:03,780
Scott: They needed the pre-funding to actually happen, right?

66
00:04:04,201 --> 00:04:12,100
Scott: And so there was lots like animation projects and things of that nature that were probably things of the day or news on GeekNights or, you know, some of them.

67
00:04:12,367 --> 00:04:12,579
Scott: Yeah.

68
00:04:12,741 --> 00:04:15,419
Scott: And it's like, and it's like, "Oh man, this thing, it'll never happen.

69
00:04:16,043 --> 00:04:18,579
Scott: But now this thing can happen that wouldn't have otherwise happened.

70
00:04:18,660 --> 00:04:19,199
Scott: I'll Kickstart that.

71
00:04:19,220 --> 00:04:20,872
Rym: You want a flashlight to annoy Rym with?

72
00:04:21,034 --> 00:04:21,798
Scott: Here you go.

73
00:04:21,899 --> 00:04:22,259
Scott: Yeah, right.

74
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:22,941
Scott: Right.

75
00:04:22,981 --> 00:04:28,060
Scott: But nowadays it's like, you can just buy Ultra Byte flashlights anywhere, right?

76
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:33,180
Scott: And so it's instead, you know, it's like the need so much for Kickstarter.

77
00:04:33,621 --> 00:04:41,079
Scott: It's like, it's still there a little bit, but it's like how many board games with a thousand fancy miniatures are there going to be?

78
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:41,641
Rym: Yeah.

79
00:04:41,661 --> 00:04:41,782
Rym: Like.

80
00:04:41,822 --> 00:04:53,380
Rym: for now, I need what like BackerKit provides because BackerKit is now basically like the place for independent creators to like manage their sales and their inventory.

81
00:04:53,901 --> 00:04:57,080
Scott: There's plenty of online e-commerce platforms you could use, right?

82
00:04:57,901 --> 00:04:59,548
Rym: Back in the day, we ran Magento for a hot minute.

83
00:05:02,840 --> 00:05:03,226
Scott: Right.

84
00:05:03,266 --> 00:05:04,179
Scott: There's tons of those.

85
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:14,839
Scott: So, you know, we've reached a point where the people who were Kickstarting things have sort of figured out how to just start a business the normal way.

86
00:05:15,640 --> 00:05:21,000
Scott: And it's not really doing the pre-funding thing as much as a pre-order thing.

87
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:28,079
Rym: I feel like the nail hit that coffin as soon as larger companies started using it literally as a promotional platform.

88
00:05:28,801 --> 00:05:38,520
Rym: And then I think the peak or the nadir, depending on your opinion on all this, was Exploding Kittens because I remember going to a conference and learning a lot about how that campaign went.

89
00:05:39,001 --> 00:05:46,460
Rym: And it was very clear that Kickstarter's pre-funding was 0% of why they used Kickstarter.

90
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:50,700
Rym: They used Kickstarter solely as a hype machine for the project.

91
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:51,601
Scott: Yeah.

92
00:05:51,641 --> 00:06:05,080
Scott: I mean, that's basically the core problem of internet discovery we talk about constantly, which is like, yeah, you want to have a distributed web where everyone has their own website, but then it's like, okay, well, how do you find anything, right?

93
00:06:05,140 --> 00:06:09,239
Scott: You can only find things when there's a place that is aggregating things.

94
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:14,079
Rym: Like Yahoo.com, which used to be a curated list of websites to look at.

95
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:18,560
Scott: But, you know, the social medias, the Googles, whatever they are, right?

96
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:23,758
Scott: There's some large singular place that you can go to search.

97
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:34,420
Scott: And then that place becomes a very powerful place, not because of anything else it does other than be a phone book or a directory or how you discover things.

98
00:06:34,601 --> 00:06:44,980
Scott: So then it becomes, you know, you have this discovery place to solve the problem of how do I find anything when there's 10 billion different websites and I have to know the domain to go to them.

99
00:06:45,201 --> 00:06:47,520
Rym: But then people need to game that system.

100
00:06:48,220 --> 00:06:56,460
Scott: Then you have the inverse problem, which is, okay, people who want to be seen now must go to those places from the other side.

101
00:06:56,720 --> 00:07:00,860
Rym: That's why in the 80s, like every fucking plumber was like AAA plumbing.

102
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:02,202
Scott: Right.

103
00:07:02,262 --> 00:07:03,679
Scott: Trying to get into the yellow pages.

104
00:07:04,002 --> 00:07:06,740
Rym: White pages and yellow pages were both gamed.

105
00:07:07,740 --> 00:07:07,981
Scott: Right.

106
00:07:08,222 --> 00:07:24,100
Scott: And so, you know, Kickstarter, which nominally was, I have an idea that I don't know if it will be a successful business, but I can get pre-funding and if enough people believe in it, at least I won't lose money making the first run and producing the thing.

107
00:07:24,782 --> 00:07:31,779
Scott: And then we'll see and be able to test whether it's a good idea without having to do it the old fashioned way of risk with taking a loan.

108
00:07:33,002 --> 00:07:33,102
Scott: Right.

109
00:07:33,203 --> 00:07:36,200
Scott: And if not enough people kickstart, well, then I don't do it.

110
00:07:36,260 --> 00:07:45,220
Scott: And I also don't take risk that I guess Kickstarter can still do that, but it hasn't done that for the majority of cases in a very long time.

111
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:57,460
Scott: It's now just another place to aggregate products, you know, to sort of, you know, promote and people come from the other side to be like, well, if I just put this on my own website, no one would see it.

112
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:00,299
Scott: So I make a Kickstarter hoping to be found.

113
00:08:00,862 --> 00:08:15,770
Rym: And I got to admit, I have come around on this specific topic of discoverability to the point that currently in the year 2024, I think big central aggregators for discoverability are too poisonous for humans in the current social environment.

114
00:08:16,233 --> 00:08:17,239
Rym: Like, I don't think they work.

115
00:08:17,921 --> 00:08:23,500
Rym: And I think the webbering might be the most highly evolved and effective form of discovery.

116
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:24,903
Scott: Well, go ahead.

117
00:08:24,963 --> 00:08:28,319
Scott: But the way I see it is that as a buyer of things, right?

118
00:08:28,824 --> 00:08:30,260
Rym: I just don't buy things anymore.

119
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:31,001
Scott: Right.

120
00:08:31,081 --> 00:08:37,460
Scott: It's like, is it really a good idea to go to a website that's always showing you new things to buy that you probably don't need?

121
00:08:37,961 --> 00:08:39,087
Scott: You know what Kickstarter turned into?

122
00:08:39,149 --> 00:08:40,780
Rym: QVC for internet people.

123
00:08:41,380 --> 00:08:41,743
Scott: Right.

124
00:08:41,803 --> 00:08:44,140
Scott: It's like, you know, you see it and it seems like a good idea.

125
00:08:44,220 --> 00:08:47,040
Scott: when you look at the Kickstarter page and you're like, oh man, that's awesome.

126
00:08:47,462 --> 00:08:50,180
Scott: And maybe you buy it and maybe it does show up at your door.

127
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:53,480
Scott: Maybe it does work as advertised and then it sits in a drawer.

128
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:59,700
Rym: I mean, between you and I, I don't know which one of us owns more indie RPGs that we have never played.

129
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:05,299
Scott: But you can now, if you want those, you go to itch.io and get a PDF instead, which is a much better idea.

130
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:09,480
Rym: So getting into some news, I got a little geek bite.

131
00:09:09,780 --> 00:09:15,231
Rym: Emily and I went to see Deadpool and Wolverine with the caveat that I haven't seen most of the MCU.

132
00:09:15,271 --> 00:09:21,600
Rym: Like, I've only seen a handful of those movies because I'm not that invested in the MCU and I got better things to do.

133
00:09:21,760 --> 00:09:24,660
Rym: But two, I haven't even seen any of the other Deadpool movies.

134
00:09:25,424 --> 00:09:34,660
Rym: So we just went to see Deadpool and Wolverine, which, if you're not aware, is now the highest grossing R-rated film in R-rated film history.

135
00:09:35,820 --> 00:09:35,920
Rym: Okay.

136
00:09:37,122 --> 00:09:45,536
Rym: You will never guess, unless you are highly tuned into politics in America, what film it beat to claim the throne.

137
00:09:45,556 --> 00:09:46,560
Rym: Do you know?

138
00:09:47,322 --> 00:09:54,100
