00:29:27,020 --> 00:29:27,925
Rym: I don't know why.

394
00:29:27,986 --> 00:29:30,259
Rym: Like, I don't feel like it was fine.

395
00:29:31,903 --> 00:29:42,200
Rym: But that aside, like, it is 12 episodes of animation science fiction that if you're a listener of GeekNights, you will probably enjoy.

396
00:29:42,820 --> 00:29:43,014
Rym: Probably.

397
00:29:43,580 --> 00:29:44,982
Rym: Because it's, I don't know, it's.

398
00:29:45,363 --> 00:29:46,204
Rym: how would I describe it?

399
00:29:46,364 --> 00:29:55,979
Rym: It's a fairly slow moving, surreal, serious science fiction story that has big Mobius vibes.

400
00:29:56,561 --> 00:29:56,821
Scott: Yeah.

401
00:29:57,243 --> 00:30:01,454
Scott: Aesthetically, it's a huge Mobius influence.

402
00:30:01,474 --> 00:30:03,700
Scott: We're talking about the comic creators Mobius, right?

403
00:30:04,580 --> 00:30:05,483
Scott: You know, it takes place.

404
00:30:05,683 --> 00:30:10,979
Scott: These people have crashed their spaceship on a planet from wherever they were coming from.

405
00:30:11,661 --> 00:30:17,200
Scott: And the planet has this crazy ecosystem that looks like a Mobius comic.

406
00:30:17,580 --> 00:30:20,594
Scott: And the people are, you know, they're not dum-dums, right?

407
00:30:20,614 --> 00:30:22,040
Scott: They're all scientists and geniuses.

408
00:30:22,181 --> 00:30:27,280
Rym: Every character acts pretty intelligently based on their deal and what they know.

409
00:30:27,380 --> 00:30:30,344
Scott: And they sort of, and you know, they're stranded.

410
00:30:30,544 --> 00:30:43,060
Scott: So they're, you know, using their knowledge and interacting with the ecosystem and understanding it and seeing how the floras and the faunas interact and how they can be useful and how they can, what can they eat and what is dangerous.

411
00:30:43,301 --> 00:30:46,920
Scott: And, you know, they haven't learned everything because they've just landed there.

412
00:30:47,381 --> 00:30:52,920
Scott: But, you know, they have advanced interstellar space travel levels of technology and smartness.

413
00:30:53,382 --> 00:30:55,854
Scott: So they can figure out more than we could, right?

414
00:30:55,954 --> 00:30:57,120
Scott: Even if we had our best technology.

415
00:30:57,180 --> 00:31:02,720
Rym: It definitely feels like alien alien slash murder bot level of interstellar travel tech.

416
00:31:02,960 --> 00:31:03,221
Scott: Right.

417
00:31:03,742 --> 00:31:10,576
Scott: You know, but yeah, the planet isn't like alien level of dangerous, right?

418
00:31:10,616 --> 00:31:12,620
Scott: But it is still quite hazardous.

419
00:31:13,040 --> 00:31:13,461
Rym: Well, you know what?

420
00:31:13,521 --> 00:31:16,405
Rym: It is alien in the most profound way.

421
00:31:16,425 --> 00:31:27,260
Rym: And that's sort of what makes, what drives this show and makes it work is that you're basically following a bunch of different characters who are all in different parts of the planet and dealing with very different situations.

422
00:31:27,701 --> 00:31:29,563
Rym: And it kind of just cuts between them all.

423
00:31:30,024 --> 00:31:36,334
Rym: But as the viewer, you can't assume anything about how this planet works.

424
00:31:36,594 --> 00:31:37,495
Rym: Like you just can't.

425
00:31:37,556 --> 00:31:40,460
Rym: You have no point of reference for how this planet works.

426
00:31:40,980 --> 00:31:45,511
Rym: The only point of reference you have are the humans and them navigating the situation.

427
00:31:45,591 --> 00:31:49,180
Rym: And if you watch these different groups navigate it, some better than others.

428
00:31:49,821 --> 00:31:49,981
Scott: Right.

429
00:31:50,021 --> 00:32:05,460
Scott: The show does a good job of a, you know, illustrating how the ecosystem and the planets work without saying you just watch and you can see, ah, I see how that, you know, these, you know, are, have a symbiotic relationship and these haven't known.

430
00:32:05,640 --> 00:32:11,428
Scott: And they also do a good job of, you know, be keeping it consistent systemically.

431
00:32:11,729 --> 00:32:11,989
Scott: Right.

432
00:32:12,269 --> 00:32:20,040
Scott: So a lot of the things you see working in the environment are Chekhov's guns that come back later and make sense in a, in a good way.

433
00:32:20,761 --> 00:32:24,427
Scott: Um, you know, and so that's like the good part of the show.

434
00:32:24,467 --> 00:32:29,356
Scott: The other good part of the show is that, you know, some of these characters are actually cool.

435
00:32:29,877 --> 00:32:31,800
Scott: The coolest being the girl with the robot.

436
00:32:33,241 --> 00:32:33,682
Rym: What's her name?

437
00:32:33,722 --> 00:32:35,387
Rym: and Levi are the best characters.

438
00:32:35,908 --> 00:32:36,149
Scott: Yes.

439
00:32:36,169 --> 00:32:36,229
Scott: Yes.

440
00:32:36,310 --> 00:32:40,000
Scott: By far you care about them and everyone else care about much less.

441
00:32:40,160 --> 00:32:46,468
Rym: Well, the guy who's the guy who interacts with the psychic little guy, not to spoil anything.

442
00:32:46,488 --> 00:32:47,049
Scott: The bad guy.

443
00:32:47,189 --> 00:32:47,389
Scott: Yeah.

444
00:32:47,529 --> 00:32:48,791
Scott: The piece of shit guy.

445
00:32:49,011 --> 00:32:50,053
Rym: That guy sucks.

446
00:32:50,113 --> 00:32:50,734
Scott: He sucks.

447
00:32:51,234 --> 00:32:53,397
Rym: And he makes bad decisions.

448
00:32:54,158 --> 00:32:54,398
Scott: Yeah.

449
00:32:54,638 --> 00:32:55,820
Scott: He's also a bad person.

450
00:32:55,920 --> 00:32:56,141
Scott: Yeah.

451
00:32:56,221 --> 00:32:59,948
Rym: But, but the extent of his badness is only revealed over the course of the show.

452
00:33:00,008 --> 00:33:02,453
Rym: Like it's at the first you're like, oh, he's being tricked.

453
00:33:02,593 --> 00:33:03,154
Rym: Sucks for him.

454
00:33:03,194 --> 00:33:05,378
Rym: And the bit by beer, like, oh, he's not just crazy.

455
00:33:05,438 --> 00:33:06,300
Rym: He's the problem.

456
00:33:07,640 --> 00:33:07,841
Scott: Yeah.

457
00:33:07,861 --> 00:33:10,525
Scott: Um, so the show starts off.

458
00:33:10,646 --> 00:33:19,100
Scott: mostly, you know, it's the first few episodes are more like the best episode of samurai Jack, the one where he has to be quiet with the archers on top of the town.

459
00:33:19,180 --> 00:33:19,581
Scott: Oh yeah.

460
00:33:20,302 --> 00:33:20,522
Scott: Right.

461
00:33:20,582 --> 00:33:26,189
Scott: Whereas, and then the show gets more, you know, get sorta gets bored of that in a way.

462
00:33:26,289 --> 00:33:28,092
Scott: And it still has it in the background.

463
00:33:28,112 --> 00:33:34,540
Scott: Like it's established the setting and it's like, okay, now that I've spent a few episodes establishing this setting, which was the best part.

464
00:33:35,040 --> 00:33:47,560
Scott: You guys, we're going to focus on these characters now and what their deal is and how, you know, their journey to get rescued or whatever they're going to do right with their precarious stranded on a remote planet situation.

465
00:33:48,161 --> 00:33:55,171
Scott: And that has, that's not bad, but it's not as interesting as, and not as exciting as the early part.

466
00:33:55,191 --> 00:33:55,411
Scott: Right.

467
00:33:56,072 --> 00:34:01,600
Scott: And then the show sorta gets to a third part where it's not really clearly like delineated.

468
00:34:01,900 --> 00:34:06,327
Scott: It's more of just like it flows from one part to the next somewhat naturally actually.

469
00:34:06,387 --> 00:34:14,679
Scott: But the third part where it's just like, okay, the characters are starting to come together, you know, uh, and interact and there's a lot more talking going on.

470
00:34:15,780 --> 00:34:29,260
Scott: And some other characters are introduced and that part was kind of not as good kind of, and it was, you know, but then the show, the thing is before that part sorta really drags you down and makes you want to stop watching the show ends.

471
00:34:29,840 --> 00:34:39,960
Scott: So if the show had started out the way it was at the end, I might've not finished it, but because it was so good and then it was like, you know, good middle.

472
00:34:41,021 --> 00:34:44,027
Scott: I won't say low, but you know, not as high as the start.

473
00:34:44,047 --> 00:34:44,770
Scott: I got to the end.

474
00:34:44,790 --> 00:34:45,672
Scott: So it does start to drag.

475
00:34:50,121 --> 00:35:01,640
Rym: And I think the main thing that I struggle with is that like we said, some of the sets of characters are just way more interesting to watch than others and just are like their stories are better stories.

476
00:35:02,181 --> 00:35:04,729
Rym: So like sometimes you're like Prince of Nothing.

477
00:35:04,749 --> 00:35:05,731
Rym: books had that problem too.

478
00:35:06,032 --> 00:35:08,680
Rym: It cycles through like all the different people doing different things.

479
00:35:09,060 --> 00:35:12,109
Scott: And usually there's like Hyperion with Canterbury Tale, right?

480
00:35:12,209 --> 00:35:16,040
Rym: It's like, yeah, the best of those, you're really invested in whatever one you're reading.

481
00:35:16,140 --> 00:35:21,140
Rym: And then when the next chapter is other people, you're like, fuck, I didn't want to jump to other people.

482
00:35:21,240 --> 00:35:28,040
Rym: But then you get two paragraphs in and you're just as invested in the other people and shows that chosen books that do that poorly.

483
00:35:28,582 --> 00:35:33,034
Rym: You really just keep, you're watching, you know, Joey Jojo getting tricked by the psychic thing.

484
00:35:33,054 --> 00:35:35,020
Rym: And you're just like, man, I want to see more Levi.

485
00:35:35,980 --> 00:35:37,142
Rym: Exactly.

486
00:35:37,663 --> 00:35:46,800
Scott: If you're going to do the shtick where you cycle through different characters, chapter to chapter or have phases, you need to make them all as good as each other.

487
00:35:47,341 --> 00:35:47,621
Scott: Right.

488
00:35:48,602 --> 00:35:55,973
Scott: Because even if you're the worst of them is good, you know, human minds evaluate things relatively right.

489
00:35:56,013 --> 00:36:00,860
Scott: Death Note had this problem and some other anime have as well where they had different arcs.

490
00:36:02,061 --> 00:36:08,180
Scott: And, you know, like Death Note is the one that stands out the most because people love the characters from the first arc so much.

491
00:36:08,921 --> 00:36:13,047
Scott: And then there's a second arc of Death Note and that has different characters in it.

492
00:36:13,628 --> 00:36:21,160
Scott: And I don't, if that was its own manga, that wasn't Death Note, it might've been moderately well received, right?

493
00:36:21,541 --> 00:36:23,529
Scott: It wasn't like an amazing manga or anything, right?

494
00:36:23,589 --> 00:36:25,276
Scott: But it was, it might've been, you know, okay.

495
00:36:26,561 --> 00:36:35,400
Scott: But because it was directly attached to that first part that was so highly esteemed and loved and wasn't as good as it, right?

496
00:36:35,801 --> 00:36:38,372
Scott: It was perceived, the second part was so disappointing.

497
00:36:38,814 --> 00:36:40,159
Scott: It got perceived as trash.

498
00:36:41,020 --> 00:36:44,527
Rym: Like, here is the fancy $400 scotch.

499
00:36:44,587 --> 00:36:46,310
Rym: Take a sip of that and you really enjoy it.

500
00:36:46,530 --> 00:36:49,416
Rym: And then they're like, all right, here's the $50 scotch.

501
00:36:49,596 --> 00:36:51,460
Rym: And you're like, oh, it's not as good as what I just drank.

502
00:36:52,241 --> 00:36:52,502
Scott: Yeah.

503
00:36:52,662 --> 00:36:56,376
Scott: But if you walked in to a normal old bar and it's like, have a $50 scotch.

504
00:36:57,520 --> 00:36:58,849
Scott: It'll be like, oh Lord, $50 scotch.

505
00:36:58,869 --> 00:36:59,192
Rym: Let's roll.

506
00:37:00,922 --> 00:37:02,268
Scott: And you'd be like, oh damn, that shit good.

507
00:37:02,770 --> 00:37:05,300
Scott: I mean, I wouldn't think it's good, but someone else might think that it's good.

508
00:37:05,660 --> 00:37:14,196
Scott: And so, you know, that's sort of what happens here is that Scavengers Reign starts out as like, you know, incredible.

509
00:37:14,516 --> 00:37:16,500
Scott: I'd say even maybe like A+ territory.

510
00:37:17,001 --> 00:37:22,640
Scott: It goes down to B territory and then it goes down to like C+ territory and then it's, and then it's over.

511
00:37:22,961 --> 00:37:26,267
Scott: But the majority of it is the A+ B territory.

512
00:37:26,769 --> 00:37:32,240
Scott: So, and by the time you get to the C territory, maybe you won't think it's C territory.

513
00:37:33,081 --> 00:37:37,358
Scott: But if you do feel the same as I do, you'll still be able to finish it because 12 episodes.

514
00:37:38,080 --> 00:37:38,180
Rym: Yeah.

515
00:37:38,601 --> 00:37:44,436
Rym: And honestly, if you're into sci-fi and you like the Mobius aesthetic, like it's a perfectly good show to watch.

516
00:37:44,497 --> 00:37:45,619
Rym: Like I do recommend it.

517
00:37:46,000 --> 00:37:52,477
Rym: But if your choice right now was, do I watch the first episode of this or do I watch Mars Express?

518
00:37:52,497 --> 00:37:53,760
Rym: Because you haven't seen that yet.

519
00:37:54,120 --> 00:37:56,439
Scott: Mars Express is going to take less time than watching 12 episodes.

520
00:37:56,700 --> 00:37:58,588
Rym: But I would also watch Mars Express first.

521
00:37:58,608 --> 00:38:01,360
Rym: Like I kind of want to watch Mars Express again already.

522
00:38:02,221 --> 00:38:03,584
Scott: Well, it costs money.

523
00:38:03,644 --> 00:38:07,433
Scott: If you did the rental like I did, you could have done the purchase or you could pirate it.

524
00:38:12,482 --> 00:38:13,766
Scott: But yeah, you know, scavengers reign.

525
00:38:13,806 --> 00:38:18,520
Scott: It's like if you have a Netflix, right, you should absolutely watch it while it's up there.

526
00:38:18,841 --> 00:38:19,303
Scott: Absolutely.

527
00:38:19,765 --> 00:38:22,480
Scott: But you shouldn't go subscribing to Netflix just to watch this.

528
00:38:22,760 --> 00:38:27,013
Scott: You should wait until there's enough other A+ amazing stuff on Netflix you haven't seen.

529
00:38:27,334 --> 00:38:29,060
Scott: Subscribe, watch all that.

530
00:38:29,420 --> 00:38:32,379
Scott: And then, yeah, also watch this and then unsubscribe from Netflix again.

531
00:38:33,741 --> 00:38:40,999
Scott: So if and when you have a Netflix subscription for another reason, definitely watch this if it's still up there.

532
00:38:42,100 --> 00:38:45,373
Scott: Otherwise, if they take it down, don't worry.

533
00:38:45,393 --> 00:38:46,055
Scott: You're not missing it.

534
00:38:46,196 --> 00:38:46,537
Rym: You'll be okay.

535
00:38:48,260 --> 00:38:54,260
Rym: The people who would particularly like this, I think, are the people who like slower, more meandering science fiction.

536
00:38:54,760 --> 00:39:01,820
Rym: Especially people who love to like see and try to figure out the deal with the science fiction world without the show explaining anything to you.

537
00:39:02,082 --> 00:39:03,579
Rym: If you like that kind of thing.

538
00:39:04,180 --> 00:39:08,820
Scott: Yeah, if you like some eco-punk kind of alien environments.

539
00:39:09,260 --> 00:39:11,586
Rym: Well, Mushishi has some similarities in that.

540
00:39:11,686 --> 00:39:17,780
Rym: Mushishi, the anime, which is fantastic, also has the dudes trying to solve problems.

541
00:39:18,100 --> 00:39:28,440
Rym: The problems are utterly alien and you don't have any frame of reference, but you learn by watching the show how to interpret this ecosystem.

542
00:39:28,621 --> 00:39:30,090
Rym: And this show does that very well.

543
00:39:30,130 --> 00:39:31,860
Rym: Like, that part is fantastic.

544
00:39:32,900 --> 00:39:32,972
Scott: Yep.

545
00:39:34,981 --> 00:39:37,047
Rym: Otherwise, the animation is good, the voice acting is good.

546
00:39:37,107 --> 00:39:41,760
Rym: Like, you know, it's a solid B science fiction, I'd say, overall.

547
00:39:42,600 --> 00:39:45,010
Scott: Yeah, then, you know, the ending isn't really too bad.

548
00:39:45,391 --> 00:39:47,519
Scott: It's a somewhat satisfying ending.

549
00:39:48,703 --> 00:39:51,513
Scott: I heard people saying they're gonna like make some more and it's like, really?

550
00:39:51,594 --> 00:39:53,099
Scott: You know, I don't know.

551
00:39:53,680 --> 00:39:56,817
Rym: Yeah, that'd be like if you heard, yeah, they're making Total Recall 2.

552
00:39:56,817 --> 00:39:57,360
Scott: Like, why?

553
00:39:58,201 --> 00:40:05,158
Scott: I think it would be a better idea rather than to make more because it's like, for me, the characters weren't the thing I cared about other than Levi.

554
00:40:07,481 --> 00:40:09,612
Scott: The robot and the girl that was with Levi.

555
00:40:09,692 --> 00:40:10,798
Scott: Also, I forget her name.

556
00:40:10,878 --> 00:40:11,180
Scott: Amazing.

557
00:40:13,963 --> 00:40:14,979
Scott: Ozzy, Ozzy, Ozzy.

558
00:40:15,381 --> 00:40:16,063
Scott: Ozzy, that's it.

559
00:40:16,183 --> 00:40:16,866
Scott: Ozzy and Levi.

560
00:40:16,906 --> 00:40:20,620
Scott: So, if they made an Ozzy-Levi show only, obviously, I'm gonna watch that.

561
00:40:20,680 --> 00:40:21,325
Scott: Oh, my God.

562
00:40:21,466 --> 00:40:23,360
Rym: I would prioritize watching that.

563
00:40:24,062 --> 00:40:28,678
Scott: But if they just continued this plot with all the characters, I'd be like, eh, maybe, right?

564
00:40:30,180 --> 00:40:37,160
Scott: What they would, I think the thing that they should do that would be the most exciting would just be to do a different planet, right?

565
00:40:37,220 --> 00:40:42,560
Scott: It's like, can you come up with a different kind of ecosystem with a different aesthetic?

566
00:40:43,281 --> 00:40:46,812
Scott: Do that same formula, except this time.

567
00:40:46,852 --> 00:40:49,200
Scott: make all the characters as cool as Ozzy and Levi.

568
00:40:49,681 --> 00:40:51,298
Scott: I only care about some of them.

569
00:40:56,883 --> 00:40:59,251
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

570
00:40:59,311 --> 00:41:01,820
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

571
00:41:02,020 --> 00:41:08,040
Scott: Be sure to visit our website at FrontRowClue.com for show notes, discussion, links and more.

572
00:41:08,540 --> 00:41:11,205
Rym: Remember, GeekNights is not one, but four different shows.

573
00:41:11,506 --> 00:41:13,149
Rym: Mondays are science and technology.

574
00:41:13,209 --> 00:41:14,952
Rym: Tuesdays, gaming and games.

575
00:41:15,072 --> 00:41:16,896
Rym: Wednesdays are animation and comics.

576
00:41:17,216 --> 00:41:19,140
Rym: Thursdays are arbitrary and indiscriminate.

577
00:41:20,420 --> 00:41:25,200
Rym: GeekNights is distributed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.

578
00:41:25,722 --> 00:41:30,020
Scott: GeekNights is recorded live with no studio and no audience.

579
00:41:30,540 --> 00:41:34,020
Scott: But unlike those other late shows, it's actually recorded at night.

580
00:41:34,500 --> 00:41:45,680
Rym: And the Patreon patrons for this episode of GeekNights are Alan Joyce, like E.G, Jared Lillithin, Brocase, Cara Makes Music, Krista Dodd, Christopher Orr, Clinton, Walton, Finn, Joel Hayes, Penny Rymer, Rekonaut, Sam Marcus, and sure about a whole bunch of people whose names I will not say.

581
00:41:47,001 --> 00:41:51,093
Rym: Our hiatus should be mostly over at this point.

582
00:41:51,254 --> 00:41:53,320
Rym: Scott's recovered from COVID, GeekNights as usual.

583
00:41:53,842 --> 00:41:58,178
Rym: But tonight, as a result, I feel like I should leave you with...

584
00:42:48,123 --> 00:42:50,513
Scott: We live on the Nile, the Nile.

585
00:42:50,573 --> 00:42:54,330
Scott: We laugh by night We sleep on the cliffs above.

586
00:42:54,370 --> 00:43:06,279
Scott: By day we fish in the deep We stand on long bare islands Of yellow sand and wet The sun sinks slowly down And the grey o'clock wolves grow dark and brown.

